00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:09.990 align:start position:0%
the protest was held exactly one year  before brazil's presidential elections  tens of thousands of brazilians from all  walks of life took to the streets of rio  de janeiro and 19 other state capitals  they may disagree on who should govern  latin america's largest economy but they  say they know who should not  the protesters danced to the same drum  beat to demand president bolsonaro's  impeachment  he is destroying the environment the  economy the democratic institutions this  government is squeezing the air out of  the people  protests against jair bolsonaro began  since he publicly denied the kovit 19  pandemic which has killed almost 600 000  brazilians to date  but now a growing number of left-wing  political parties have joined forces  with trade unions and social  organizations  even if they don't see eye-to-eye they  have a common goal  brazil is politically polarized polls  show 46 percent of the population  supporting former president luis  signiasso lula da silva from the leftist  workers party and 25 percent in favor of  bolsonaro  that leaves 30 percent of brazilians  still looking for a candidate from the  center  but polls also indicate that 59 percent  of brazilians reject bolsonaro  an impeachment is unlikely to happen  because although there are enough  reasons to force bolsonaro out he still  has support in congress but it's  important to show the country that the  majority of brazilians want him out  and these protesters say  that can only be achieved by making  their voices heard out on the streets  manuka yanakiv al-jazeera  [Music]
 

